{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Thread Safety in JNI Function Calls","content":"# Thread Safety in JNI Function Calls\n\n# What is JNI development?[ #](#what-is-jni-development)\n\nI used to do this kind of work, and after a former colleague raised some questions, I set aside time to dig into JNI development issues and one specific detail — thread safety of calls. JNI-layer calls are not that different from other languages calling into C/C++ — for example CGO between Go and C/C++ shares many of the same concerns.\n\nJNI is Java’s native interface. With C/C++ (or other languages), compiled code inside the JVM can interact with native code outside it.\n\n> Java emphasizes portability. Native code looks like it breaks that promise, but it is often unavoidable — Java cannot implement every low-level capability.\n\n# What tools do you need for JNI?[ #](#what-tools-do-you-need-for-jni)\n\nOn my machine I run OpenJDK 11 and GCC 11 on Linux. For editing I use VS Code with the Java, CMake, and C++ extensions.\n\nToolchain:\n\n* OpenJDK 11\n* GCC 11\n* CMake 3.21\n* Latest VS Code\n\nScreenshot:\n\n![vscode](/post/jnithreadsafe/8025a99f2589.png)\n\n> Why can’t OpenJDK find `javah`? See [JEP 313](http://openjdk.java.net/jeps/313): `javah` was removed. `javac` can do the same job now:\n>\n> shellCopy\n>\n> ```shell\n> javac -cp . -h abc MyCls.java\n> ```\n>\n> Assuming `MyCls.java` in the current directory has native methods, this creates an `abc` directory with the C++ JNI header. You then write the matching C++ source.\n\n# Terminology[ #](#terminology)\n\nYou can look up **thread safety** anywhere, and you may already know it, but here is a quick restatement:\n\nA function is thread-safe if, when called concurrently from multiple threads, every caller still gets a correct result.\n\n# Main factors[ #](#main-factors)\n\nThread-safety problems appear when **a call must mutate shared state**.\n\nThat covers four cases:\n\n* Static Java native call mutating shared C++ data\n* Static Java native call mutating shared Java data\n* Instance Java native call mutating shared C++ data\n* Instance Java native call mutating shared Java data\n\nAny shared data can create thread-safety issues.\n\n> Static vs instance native calls: if the native method is `static`, it is a class-level call; otherwise it is an instance call.\n>\n> Shared C++ data means globals (or other globally reachable state). Shared Java data here means fields on a Java class or object that C++ reads or writes through JNI.\n\n# Walkthrough[ #](#walkthrough)\n\nBelow is a small demo of JNI thread-safety issues.\n\n## Java side[ #](#java-side)\n\nThe Java side is simple: declare a few native methods and call them from `main`:\n\njavaCopy\n\n```java\nclass MyCls {\n  public static void main(String[] args) {\n    if (loadLibrary()) {\n      sayHello();\n\n      ConSt st = new ConSt();\n\n      threadSafe(st); // thread-safe as long as the passed-in st is unique per caller\n\n      System.out.println(mySafeInt);\n      System.out.println(st.mInt);\n\n      System.out.printf(\"unsafe int:%d\\n\", threadUnsafe());\n\n      MyCls obj = new MyCls();\n      obj.threadUnsafe2();\n      System.out.printf(\"unsafe int:%d\\n\", obj.myUnsafeInt);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Load the C shared library\n  private static boolean loadLibrary() {\n    try {\n      System.loadLibrary(\"abc\");\n    } catch(SecurityException e) {\n      e.printStackTrace();\n      return false;\n    } catch(UnsatisfiedLinkError e) {\n      e.printStackTrace();\n      return false;\n    }\n    return true;\n  }\n\n  private static long mySafeInt = 0;\n  private long myUnsafeInt = 0;\n\n  // Native interfaces\n  private static native void threadSafe(ConSt st);\n  private static native int threadUnsafe(); // uses shared C++ state with no locking\n  private native void threadUnsafe2(); // uses shared Java state with no locking\n}\n```\n\nThe heart of the demo is these three natives. `threadSafe()` could be split into two functions; in the C++ below I kept them together for brevity.\n\n`threadSafe()` remains correct under any number of concurrent calls. `threadUnsafe()` and `threadUnsafe2()` are not thread-safe — under many threads you get scrambled results.\n\n## C/C++ side[ #](#cc-side)\n\nc++Copy\n\n```c++\nstd::mutex mu_thread_safe;\n\n/*\n * Class:     MyCls\n * Method:    threadSafe\n * Signature: (LConSt;)V\n */\nJNIEXPORT void JNICALL Java_MyCls_threadSafe\n  (JNIEnv *env, jclass clz, jobject cons_st) {\n    // -- 1 -- Use a C++ mutex so the mutation is thread-safe\n    jfieldID _fieldId = env->GetStaticFieldID(clz, \"mySafeInt\", \"J\"); // static field on the class\n\n    mu_thread_safe.lock();\n    env->SetStaticLongField(clz, _fieldId, 12345); // mutex makes this safe\n    mu_thread_safe.unlock();\n\n    // -- 2 -- Pass state via a formal parameter; unique objects keep it thread-safe\n    jclass _cs_clz = env->FindClass(\"ConSt\");\n    assert(_cs_clz != nullptr);\n    jfieldID _cs_fieldId = env->GetFieldID(_cs_clz, \"mInt\", \"J\");\n    env->SetLongField(cons_st, _cs_fieldId, 12345);\n  }\n\n/*\n * Class:     MyCls\n * Method:    threadUnsafe\n * Signature: ()V\n */\nJNIEXPORT jint JNICALL Java_MyCls_threadUnsafe\n  (JNIEnv *, jclass) {\n    // -- 3 -- Shared C++ state mutated without locking — not thread-safe\n    static int _unsafe_int = 0;\n    _unsafe_int++; // no mutex\n    return _unsafe_int;\n  }\n\n/*\n * Class:     MyCls\n * Method:    threadUnsafe2\n * Signature: ()V\n */\nJNIEXPORT void JNICALL Java_MyCls_threadUnsafe2\n  (JNIEnv *env, jobject obj) {\n    // -- 4 -- Shared Java field mutated from C++ without locking — also not thread-safe\n    jclass _clz = env->FindClass(\"MyCls\");\n    assert(_clz != nullptr);\n    jfieldID fieldId = env->GetFieldID(_clz, \"myUnsafeInt\", \"J\");\n    assert(fieldId != nullptr);\n    jlong myUnsafeInt = env->GetLongField(obj, fieldId) + 1;\n    env->SetLongField(obj, fieldId, myUnsafeInt);\n  }\n```\n\nIn the first function, marker 1 uses a C++ mutex — that works, but under heavy concurrency it serializes everything and hurts throughput. Marker 2 is usually preferable: the caller passes a custom object, C++ mutates that object, and as long as each caller’s object is unique, concurrent results stay correct.\n\nMarkers 3 and 4 are counterexamples. Marker 3 mutates shared C++ state without locking; marker 4 is similar but the shared state lives in a Java field.\n\n> What is the fourth `sign` argument to `GetFieldID()`? It is the JNI type signature for that field. See the figure below (a screenshot from elsewhere); the official docs have the full table.\n>\n> ![sign](/post/jnithreadsafe/327d2bc302b0.png)\n\n# Summary[ #](#summary)\n\nThread safety means using **shared resources** carefully. Prefer not sharing at all — let callers pass the values to mutate. That usually works best.\n\nLinks: demo [download](../zh/jnithreadsafe/src.tar.gz)\n","locale":"en","slug":"jnithreadsafe"}}